 WHAT IT DOES
 ------------

 When DOS writes a file it takes whatever free clusters it finds first.
 After a few months of creating, growing and deleting files, most of your
 files are scattered in pieces all over the disk, and the drive head spends
 its life seeking back and forth.  Programs load slower, the disk wears
 faster, and an UNDELETE is less likely to work.

 The ARM Disk Optimizer (DEFRAG.EXE) puts every file back together in one
 contiguous piece and, if you like, packs all the files at the front of the
 disk so the free space is one big area.  You watch it work on a map of
 the whole disk.

 It works with every ARM-DOS drive formatted with a 12-bit or 16-bit FAT:
 360K, 720K, 1.2M and 1.44M diskettes and hard disk partitions up to the
 largest DOS 4 allows.


 BEFORE YOU START
 ----------------

   1. BACK UP YOUR DISK.  The optimizer is careful (see "Safety" below),
      but a power failure is a power failure.

   2. Run CHKDSK /F first.  The optimizer refuses to work on a disk with
      lost clusters or cross-linked files.

   3. Close all other programs.  Do not run the optimizer from a DOSSHELL
      or other program that has files open, and do not run it on a drive
      that a network, SUBST, JOIN or ASSIGN has redirected.


 INSTALLATION
 ------------

 Copy DEFRAG.EXE to your DOS directory:

      COPY DEFRAG.EXE C:\DOS

 That's all.  No configuration file, no TSR, nothing in AUTOEXEC.BAT.


 RUNNING IT
 ----------

      DEFRAG [d:] [/F | /U] [/S[:]order[-]] [/H] [/FAST] [/AUTO] [/BW]

      d:      the drive to optimize (asked for if you leave it out)
      /F      Full Optimization
      /U      Unfragment Files Only
      /S      sort the files in each directory:
                 N  by name          D  by date and time
                 E  by extension     S  by size
              a "-" after the letter reverses the order (/S:D- = newest
              first)
      /H      also move hidden files (system files never move)
      /FAST   run at full speed instead of the leisurely pace of a real
              disk
      /AUTO   ask nothing: analyze, optimize, exit (for batch files; the
              exit code is 0 done, 1 error, 2 stopped)
      /BW     black and white (for LCD and composite screens)

 Without /F, /U or /AUTO the optimizer first reads the disk and tells you
 how much of it is fragmented and which method it recommends.  Choose
 Optimize to go ahead, or Configure to pick things yourself from the
 Optimize menu (Alt or F10):

      Begin Optimization    starts (Alt+B)
      Drive                 picks another drive
      Optimization Method   Full Optimization or Unfragment Files Only
      File Sort             the order of the files in each directory
      Map Legend            what the symbols on the map mean
      Fast Speed            no pacing (check mark = on)
      About Defrag          version and copyright
      Exit                  back to DOS (Alt+X)

 A mouse works everywhere: click the menu, the choices and the buttons.
 Press Esc during an optimization to stop it; it stops cleanly after the
 piece it is moving and the disk is always left in a usable state.


 THE MAP
 -------

      (full block)   used: part of a file or directory
      (shaded)       unused: free space
      r              being read
      W              being written
      B              bad clusters (never used)
      X              unmovable: IO.SYS, ARMDOS.SYS, system and hidden
                     files, files other programs have open

 On a big disk each block stands for several clusters; the Legend box
 says how many.


 THE TWO METHODS
 ---------------

 FULL OPTIMIZATION rewrites the whole disk: directories first, then the
 files, directory by directory, packed from the start of the disk with no
 gaps.  Afterwards every file is in one piece and all the free space is at
 the end, so new files will not be fragmented either.  Takes longer.

 UNFRAGMENT FILES ONLY moves each fragmented file, whole, into the first
 free area big enough to hold it.  Quicker, but the free space stays in
 pieces.  If a file does not fit anywhere it is left alone; run Full
 Optimization.


 SAFETY
 ------

 Every piece of a file is copied to free space and read back and compared
 BEFORE the file allocation table is changed.  Then the one link that
 leads to the piece (the directory entry or the previous cluster) is
 switched over, and only then is the old copy freed.  Every FAT copy is
 updated each time.  If the power fails in the middle, the worst you get
 is a few lost clusters, which CHKDSK /F turns into FILE0000.CHK files.

 IO.SYS and ARMDOS.SYS are never moved: the boot record expects IO.SYS to
 start at the first cluster in one piece.  System files, hidden files
 (unless /H) and files that DOS has open stay where they are too.

 Sorting the directories rewrites directory sectors and drops the entries
 of deleted files, so UNDELETE cannot bring those back afterwards.


